Subject: [PATCH 11361/11362] The compaction A/B already existed and the ledger
 said evidence: []

Asked to run an A/B of CARNOT_ARC_INDUCE_TOOL_COMPACT on the 27B. Three
findings landed before any GPU time was spent, and together they made a new
run the wrong thing to buy. No GPU was used. Nothing under results/ was
written.

1. The briefed A/B cannot run. arc_induction_tool_loop.py:768 REFUSES
   grammar and compaction together (REQ-7044, terminated_by=
   grammar_compaction_unsupported). The loop executes zero turns and falls
   back to single-shot induction. Holding the grammar fixed in both arms
   would have compared "tool loop" against "no tool loop" and called the
   difference compaction. Verified live: the interlock test passes.

2. At the trials' 4-turn shape compaction never fires. Driving the shipped
   CompactionController over the measured prompt-token sequences from both
   27B grammar trials gives 0 events in 14 of 14 cells at the default growth
   of 8192. Largest growth over turn 0 was 8427, which crosses only on the
   final measurement, so a 5th turn would have been needed to act on it.

3. results/tool_loop_compaction_ab_20260820/ holds a complete 13-cell paired
   A/B from 2026-08-20, on a compaction module and wiring block that are
   byte-identical to HEAD. It fires 10 times over 7 of 13 cells. The ledger
   recorded it as evidence: [], state: unevaluated, for 17 days.

The verdict from that A/B, now recorded: the mechanism works and buys
nothing measurable. Peak prompt tokens fall 26.3% on the cells that fire,
and 50.4% on the one cell where both arms ran 12 turns. Holdout accuracy
ties on all 11 paired scoreable cells -- but 21 of 23 scored cells sit at
exactly 0.0, so the metric was floored and the tie says nothing
(FALSE_NEGATIVE_RISK). Median cell wall clock got worse, 650.9s to 722.1s,
failing the design's own G-W gate; most of that is the ON arm running 7 more
turns because compaction suppressed early stops, not a per-turn slowdown.
Parse safety passed. Candidates scored fell 34 to 27 while turns rose 96 to
103. G-K, the concurrency gate the design calls its primary lever, is still
unmeasured.

Two checks worth keeping. The six cells that did not fire are byte-identical
across arms, which is a free A/A validation that the flag is inert until the
trigger crosses. And the decode-rate curve the design rests on was measured
from the A/B's own server logs: it is real but shallow in the 6.7k-41.6k
band these loops occupy, -11.7% end to end, and the ON arm decoded slower at
every matched context bucket, which cancels the gain. That penalty is an
unexplained confound of the sequential arm design, not a property of
compaction.

Instrument audit: a first cross-check of prompt_tokens_per_turn against the
server log reported a mismatch. The check was wrong, not the data -- under
cache_prompt the server logs the delta prefilled, not the full prompt. The
exact release-line identity reconstructs all 56 turn measurements across
both trials. The replay's falsification control is the growth sweep: the
same instrument reports 14 events at growth=1024 and 0 at 8192, so it can
fire and did not.

Ledger: COMPACT -> off_measured with both evidence entries and their
sha256s. GROWTH and STATE_BUDGET stay unevaluated -- neither was ever varied
in a live run -- with what was observed at their defaults recorded so the
next reader does not re-derive it. No benchmark_reachable was added.

